&ldquo;Now why wouldn&rsquo;t you share with Twitter that you went to see Kanye 2 nights in a row??&rdquo; the user posted, tagging O&rsquo;Day. &ldquo;All that hootin and hollerin about Diddy&rsquo;s abuse, but you fail to keep that same energy with Kanye.&rdquo;

O&rsquo;Day has s poken out against Didd y for years. Danity Kane was formed on Diddy&rsquo;s series Making the Band and signed to his label Bad Boy Records. O&rsquo;Day was kicked out of the group in 2008, and in an appearance on Call Her Daddy , she alleged that the falling out happened because she refused to comply with non-musical requests from the rapper. In the years since, O&rsquo;Day has continued to speak out against Diddy, including in the recent Netflix documentary Sean Combs: The Reckoning .

To X users, O&rsquo;Day&rsquo;s support of Ye stands in direct opposition to her outspokenness against Diddy. The original X post ended by calling the singer a &ldquo;f&mdash;ing hypocrite.&rdquo;

Rather than ignoring the post, O&rsquo;Day replied with a defense of her decision to see Ye.

&ldquo;I can hold two truths at once,&rdquo; she starts before saying that she&rsquo;s lived through and does not excuse abuse. She then says that she does not believe that &ldquo;engaging with someone&rsquo;s art&rdquo; is the same as &ldquo;[co-signing] every opinion or action they&rsquo;ve ever had.&rdquo; O&rsquo;Day says that if this were the case, many people in the entertainment industry would not have careers and that there is more &ldquo;nuance&rdquo; to these situations. &ldquo;If supporting art required endorsing every belief of the artist, none of us would have careers, or playlists.&rdquo;

I can hold two truths at once.
I&rsquo;ve been vocal about abuse because I&rsquo;ve lived it, and I don&rsquo;t excuse it, ever. That hasn&rsquo;t changed. But I also don&rsquo;t believe engaging with someone&rsquo;s art means I co-sign every opinion or action they&rsquo;ve ever had. If that were the rule, most of this&hellip; https://t.co/ThOn6iG5A8

&mdash; Aubrey O'Day (@AubreyODay) April 6, 2026

This explanation was not enough for people who do not agree with her stance, with another X user replying to her post by calling her a hypocrite and saying &ldquo;by your logic we should ignore all of the things you said about Puffy because he makes good music.&rdquo;

O&rsquo;Day again took to the social platform to double down on her statement, explaining that a &ldquo;blanket &lsquo;cancel everything&rsquo; stance&rdquo; is not realistic for her as an artist and that the user she is replying to is &ldquo;not accounting for real-world complexity.&rdquo;

&ldquo;The idea that you have to erase art to prove morality, or perform constant outrage to stay &lsquo;consistent,&rsquo; isn&rsquo;t integrity..it&rsquo;s a simplified version of ethics,&rdquo; she continues. &ldquo;I&rsquo;m allowed to hold nuance.&rdquo;

The singer then says that she has remained consistent on her position on Diddy for 20 years and had her life and career negatively impacted by doing so &ldquo;in ways you&rsquo;ll never fully understand.&rdquo; She concludes by saying she will no longer be &ldquo;outsourcing my values to a comment section anymore.&rdquo;

In another response, she wrote: &ldquo;You&rsquo;re pushing a binary moral model-good people get total support, bad people get total rejection. Real life doesn&rsquo;t work like that. Humans hold complexity all the time.. we love people who&rsquo;ve hurt us, we consume work from imperfect creators. We live in nuance and still know exactly where our values stand.&rdquo;

Although she claimed to be done interacting with comments, O&rsquo;Day replied to a few more users who questioned her position. In these follow-up interactions, she continued to reference &ldquo;nuance&rdquo; and push her idea that engagement does not mean endorsement. In one reply , she brought up the Epstein Files, saying, &ldquo;A lot of $ in the Epstein files funded a lot of sh&ndash; that you like.&rdquo;

O&rsquo;Day eventually concluded the conversation on X with a standalone post , not responding directly to anyone anymore.

&ldquo;Most people want clean heroes & clean villains, because it&rsquo;s easier. I&rsquo;ve seen too much in my lifetime to simplify people in that manner,&rdquo; she wrote. &ldquo;I understand the complexity, and I choose every day how I engage with it consciously.&rdquo;

O&rsquo;Day&rsquo;s X back-and-forth defense of Ye happened the same day the rapper&rsquo;s appearance at London&rsquo;s Wireless Festival was thwarted by the British government when the entity denied his entry into the country , citing his history of antisemitism. As a result, the festival &mdash; which previously lost four corporate sponsors when Ye&rsquo;s headlining spot on the lineup was announced &mdash; was completely canceled .
